# Heavy binary assets go to LFS. Git stores compressed deltas, and a `.glb` or a
# `.png` is already compressed — so a normal commit of one is the whole file
# again, kept forever. A pointer is 130 bytes.
#
# `-text` on every line: these are binary and must never be line-ending
# normalised. A `.glb` that goes through CRLF conversion is a corrupt `.glb`.
*.glb     filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.fbx     filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.blend   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.meshlib filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Textures under `game/assets/`, which is where they arrive in bulk. The handful
# in `game/presentation/` are a few kilobytes each and are better off as ordinary
# blobs — a pointer file is friction for anything that has to read one without
# LFS installed, and a 130-byte pointer for a 144-byte shading ramp is not a
# saving.
#
# **The boundary is the folder, not the subfolder.** This said
# `game/assets/species/**` and the other two folders beside it fell through: a
# one-megabyte logo and an 876-kilobyte character texture were committed whole.
# `game/assets/` is the line the comment above was always describing, so it is
# the line the rule draws now — a texture put in a new folder there is covered
# the day it arrives rather than the day somebody notices.
game/assets/**/*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
game/assets/**/*.jpg filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
